{"downloaded": true, "htmlmade": false, "id": "40004981", "source": "MED", "pmid": "40004981", "pmcid": "PMC11858096", "fullTextIdList": {"fullTextId": "PMC11858096"}, "doi": "10.3390/nu17040653", "title": "The Potential Role of Phytochemicals in Alzheimer's Disease.", "authorString": "Baranowska-W\u00f3jcik E, Gajowniczek-A\u0142asa D, Pawlikowska-Pawl\u0119ga B, Szwajgier D.", "authorList": {"author": [{"fullName": "Baranowska-W\u00f3jcik E", "firstName": "Ewa", "lastName": "Baranowska-W\u00f3jcik", "initials": "E", "authorId": {"@type": "ORCID", "#text": "0000-0002-2213-9631"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": {"affiliation": "Department of Biotechnology, Microbiology and Human Nutrition, University of Life Sciences, Skromna Street 8, 20-704 Lublin, Poland."}}}, {"fullName": "Gajowniczek-A\u0142asa D", "firstName": "Dorota", "lastName": "Gajowniczek-A\u0142asa", "initials": "D", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": {"affiliation": "Department of Biotechnology, Microbiology and Human Nutrition, University of Life Sciences, Skromna Street 8, 20-704 Lublin, Poland."}}}, {"fullName": "Pawlikowska-Pawl\u0119ga B", "firstName": "Bo\u017cena", "lastName": "Pawlikowska-Pawl\u0119ga", "initials": "B", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": {"affiliation": "Department of Functional Anatomy and Cytobiology, Institute of Biological Sciences, Faculty of Biology and Biotechnology, Maria Curie-Sklodowska University, Akademicka 19, 20-033 Lublin, Poland."}}}, {"fullName": "Szwajgier D", "firstName": "Dominik", "lastName": "Szwajgier", "initials": "D", "authorId": {"@type": "ORCID", "#text": "0000-0002-2285-0669"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": {"affiliation": "Department of Biotechnology, Microbiology and Human Nutrition, University of Life Sciences, Skromna Street 8, 20-704 Lublin, Poland."}}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"@type": "ORCID", "#text": "0000-0002-2213-9631"}, {"@type": "ORCID", "#text": "0000-0002-2285-0669"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": "altmetrics"}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "4", "volume": "17", "journalIssueId": "3905669", "dateOfPublication": "2025 Feb", "monthOfPublication": "2", "yearOfPublication": "2025", "printPublicationDate": "2025-02-01", "journal": {"title": "Nutrients", "ISOAbbreviation": "Nutrients", "medlineAbbreviation": "Nutrients", "NLMid": "101521595", "ISSN": "2072-6643", "ESSN": "2072-6643"}}, "pubYear": "2025", "pageInfo": "653", "abstractText": "Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a neurodegenerative condition characterised by memory loss and cognitive disorders. The disease has been related to the presence of so-called senile plaques forming due to the buildup of amyloid \u03b2 in the hippocampus. The AD therapies developed to date continue to prove insufficient, while long-term exposure to synthetic drugs tends to lead to serious side effects, which is why potential herbal treatments are generally preferable to conventional drug regimens and, as such, have been under considerable research scrutiny in recent years. There are a number of herbs, e.g., lavender <i>Ginkgo biloba</i>, that are already commonly employed in alleviating the symptoms of certain neurological disorders. In light of the above, the aim of the following paper is to discuss the importance of medicinal herbs, their neuroprotective properties, and their mechanisms of activity. The article presents a review of the identified therapeutic properties of phytomedicines that exhibit strong anti-Alzheimer's disease (AD) activity.", "affiliation": "Department of Biotechnology, Microbiology and Human Nutrition, University of Life Sciences, Skromna Street 8, 20-704 Lublin, Poland.", "publicationStatus": "epublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["review-article", "Review", "Journal Article"]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Animals"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName